{# The request classifier node, for deployments with no scope catalogue.

   Its catalogue-backed twin is rag/scope_check.j2, which can show the model real
   indexed questions and their similarity scores. This one has no such evidence — it
   judges against a prose description of what the deployment covers — so it leans
   harder on explicit rules and worked examples, and it states the cost asymmetry for
   each decision, because a classifier with weaker evidence is one more likely to
   guess.

   THREE DECISIONS IN ONE CALL. Scope, disclosed profile fields, and whether the
   message is about the caller's own child — and, when it is, what answering it would
   have to read. Asking separately would triple the cost of the one call every turn
   already pays, and the three share all of their context: the same message, the same
   history, the same description of the deployment.

   That last part rides along with the child decision rather than becoming a fourth
   call for the same reason: it is a refinement of an answer the model has already
   worked out, and it is only ever asked when `about_child` is true. It decides which
   TOOLS the answering agent is given, so its safe value is `both` — see
   CHILD_QUESTION_KINDS in backend/chat/signals.py for why every failure lands there.

   ORDER IS FOR CACHING, the same rule agent/system.j2 states and for the same reason.
   Everything fixed per deployment comes first — the role, the coverage description,
   every rule and every example — and the two things that vary per turn, the
   conversation and the message, come last. Providers cache on a shared prefix, so
   moving the message upward would invalidate roughly two thousand tokens of rules on
   every single turn. This is why the message appears at the bottom under a heading
   rather than inline where each decision refers to it.

   The child section is rendered only when the deployment has children to talk about,
   so a document knowledge base never pays a token for it.

   The classifier is NEVER told who is asking. It reports what the MESSAGE says;
   whether this caller may act on that is decided afterwards by code that has verified
   a signature. A classifier that knew the caller could be talked into changing its
   answer about them.

   Values are passed in as render arguments and never re-rendered, so a persona or a
   coverage description containing {{ ... }} is data, not template. #}
You are a request classifier. You do not answer the user and you never talk to them — you read one message and report facts about it as structured output. Something else decides what to do with your answer.

The message you are classifying appears at the end, under "THE MESSAGE". Read these rules first.

